**Amendment (Decoder evidence)** · `amend:devnet:wallet-decoder-evidence` The pn-wallet library implements bounded public layout decoding and explicit evidence selection under (`amend:devnet:wallet-decoder`), (`amend:devnet:wallet-case-census`) and (`verif:devnet:wallet-layout`). Explicit 32-byte nonce carriage is a valid non-evidence layout: prefix 1 is retained in both the adapter and the independent reader, and a block containing it still exposes its separate qualifying evidence output. The independent test reader is authored from the field layout, imports neither elements nor the adapter and compares every exposed field, including confidential, issuance, peg-in and witness carriage. Its parsing of commitments is opaque and prefix-checked; it makes no cryptographic verification claim. The exact run/target/request OP_RETURN fixture is a local selector witness, not a real-chain relay-policy admission. The malformed-input cases cover count prefixes, incomplete and trailing bytes, flags, script and witness limits, and invalid C-parsed encodings. Exhibits: ``[WALLET-claim:wallet:full-layouts-preserve-nonevidence-transactions]``, ``[WALLET-claim:wallet:explicit-evidence-binds-target-run-and-request]``, ``[WALLET-claim:wallet:hostile-prefixes-refuse-before-codec-allocation]``, ``[WALLET-claim:wallet:invalid-curve-encodings-refuse]``, ``[WALLET-claim:wallet:bounded-decoder-campaign-retains-layouts]`` and ``[WALLET-claim:wallet:decoder-reservations-have-measured-bounds]``. Broader real-chain recognition and settlement remain held under (`open:design:devnet-evidence`).

**Amendment (Heap testing)** · `amend:devnet:wallet-heap-testing` dhat 0.3.3 is pinned for pn-wallet tests only, with notices in packages/wallet/NOTICE-DEPENDENCIES. Each measurement child creates one heap profiler, drops the decoded value before reading statistics and stays within the fixed finite fixture sizes. dhat forwards the System allocation contract; successful live allocations are tracked under its lock, reentrant profiler allocations bypass measurement, and allocation failure returns before count updates. Heap-mode and live-block assertions hold under this single-profiler use; totals remain far below overflow. No first-party unsafe code is added. The measured peaks are 0 bytes for maximal-count short input, over-limit input and the malformed maximum-width prefix; 720,896 bytes for 1,024 complete inputs; and 10,926 bytes for nested witnesses, against each vector's computed envelope of sixteen times encoded bytes plus 15,360 times declared entries plus 4,194,304 bytes, rather than merely the hard allocation cap. Requested totals are respectively 0, 0, 0, 1,368,016 and 17,495 bytes, with zero retained bytes after drop. The proof-bearing transaction requested 28,322 bytes and peaked at 22,524; its containing block requested 28,456 and peaked at 22,580; the dynafed block requested 3,046 and peaked at 1,790. A block of exactly 4,000,000 bytes and 4,096 aggregate entries requested 14,054,628 bytes and peaked at 6,707,691, below its computed 131,108,864-byte envelope. The additional 4,094-output vector carries a parsed surjection proof on every output, together with one input and one transaction: 4,096 aggregate entries in 692,023 bytes. It requests 36,638,588 bytes and peaks at 34,816,283 bytes, below its 78,181,232-byte computed envelope, exercising the 15,360-byte entry charge with native proof storage at scale. All retained zero after drop; a truncated block requested zero. These fixture measurements are not a proof for every byte string or a process-memory limit. The graph witness (`claim:discipline:wallet-development-edges-stay-out-of-production`) checks each workspace member against its recorded normal/build package closure under default and disabled defaults and every combination of its declared features. This excludes dev-only and detached fuzz packages from production owners while retaining the testkit's own reviewed normal graph. Negative graph witnesses cover the profiler, property engine, fuzz runtime, arbitrary generator and fuzz package; host defaults also exclude the wallet.

**Amendment (Decoder robustness campaign)** · `amend:devnet:wallet-fuzz-campaign` The detached pn-wallet-fuzz target calls both public decoder entry points with fixed byte, item, script, witness and reservation limits. A bounded campaign uses cargo-fuzz 0.13.2 with nightly-2026-10-16 and the address sanitizer, including address instrumentation for the C build. A campaign names the tree it exercised and, once imported, the public commit holding that tree, because signing changes commit identity without changing source content. The renewed differential campaign exercised tree 052127c516182b8c4c55c814f47627a67eef1cc6, held in public commit 289b6e3e043ce26c277d8e21a1edc164ce553d76 (`fix(wallet): compare codec acceptance within reachable limits`), starting with twelve committed malformed-input and valid-layout seeds in fresh corpus and artifact directories. With a 120-second requested limit and 4,000,000-byte maximum input length, it completed 1,946,343 executions in 121 libFuzzer seconds (124.983 seconds for the command including incremental compilation), with coverage 2,821 and 9,343 features, no disagreement, no crash and no sanitizer report. These are instrumented counters, not a coverage percentage or proof of completeness. The same source passed 256 configured and completed differential property cases in the full gate with the fuzzing feature enabled. Corpus growth and artifacts remain operational evidence outside the tree. The non-default fuzzing feature exposes a hidden bare-codec verdict and first-party projection inside the sole adapter. The gate enables this feature for the registered differential property; production defaults do not. The comparison checks resource eligibility from decoded codec fields independently of preflight and requires equal acceptance and projection within those limits. Fixtures include explicit nonces, the pinned codec archive's dynafed block and its proof-bearing 1in2out transaction; the two upstream fixtures retain that archive's notice and are not a new real-chain recognition claim. The ordinary recorded property tier exercises the independent reader as well as bounded arbitrary bytes ``[WALLET-claim:wallet:bounded-decoder-campaign-retains-layouts]``. No sanitized-standard-library failure was observed. Future decoder changes require renewed campaign evidence.

**Amendment (Case join)** · `amend:devnet:wallet-case-join` The 53 cases of (`amend:devnet:wallet-case-census`) are joined by ci/wallet_cases.py to explicit held remainders or test-site claims and their executed results. Missing, duplicate or stale cases, absent source claims, unsuccessful execution and discharge with a held case refuse. The dependency identity check reads Cargo metadata, including renamed packages; the exact reviewed binding features are compared, and an adapter path or re-export outside the confined module refuses. Exhibits: (`claim:discipline:wallet-census-refuses-missing-evidence`), (`claim:discipline:wallet-codec-paths-stay-confined`) and (`claim:discipline:wallet-development-edges-stay-out-of-production`). Crypto admission remains scoped by (`amend:crypto:wallet-parsing-admission`); the later service cases stay held.

